# Synthetic credential strings — attested as non-secret
#
# The pre-push guard blocks credential-shaped strings. This repository's test suite must
# contain such strings in order to test that redaction works, so each one is listed here
# explicitly rather than blanket-excepting the test directory — a blanket exception would
# let a real secret hide in exactly the place most likely to be skimmed.
#
# Every value below is fake. They are either the documentation examples from the respective
# providers (AWS's own AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE) or obviously-sequential canaries invented for
# these tests. None has ever authenticated anything.
#
# Adding a line here is a claim that the string is synthetic. Review it the same way you
# would review the code introducing it.

# AWS's documented example key id, used by every SDK's own docs.
AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE
ASIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE

# Canary in test/adapter.test.ts — the stand-in for a real credential in adapter tests.
ghp_TESTTOKENabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123

# Neon-shaped canaries in test/neon.test.ts, test/config.test.ts and
# test/conformance.test.ts — same contract as the GitHub canary above: synthetic bodies
# developed for these suites only, kept provider-prefixed so the response scanners and
# configuration sweeps have something to catch. Never authenticated against anything.
napi_TESTTOKENabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123
napi_TESTFIXTUREabcdef0123456789ghij

# Canary in test/mcp.test.ts — asserts the logger's redaction actually fires.
ghp_ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ012345

# Slack-shaped canary in test/adapter.test.ts, exercising the response scanner.
xoxb-1234567890-abcdefghij